final_answer:
I have everything I need. The bug is fully understood. Here is the complete, precise diagnosis and fix.

---

## Root Cause Analysis

There are **two distinct but compounding bugs**, both introduced during the refactoring of the data persistence layer. Together they cause every post saved as `DRAFT` to be immediately visible as `PUBLISHED`.

---

### Bug 1 — `prisma/schema.prisma`, `Post` model, `isPublished` field (line 123)

**File:** `prisma/schema.prisma`

```prisma
// CURRENT (buggy)
isPublished   Boolean    @default(false) @map("is_published")
```

Wait — the schema default is actually `false` here, which is correct on its own. The real problem is in how the repository mapper interacts with the Prisma field name. Read on.

---

### Bug 1 — `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`, `PostMapper.toPrismaCreate` (lines 72–75)

**File:** `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`
**Function:** `PostMapper.toPrismaCreate`

```typescript
// CURRENT (buggy)
toPrismaCreate(data: Post): Prisma.PostCreateInput {
  const { id, ...rest } = data;
  return { id, ...rest };
},
```

This mapper blindly spreads the entire domain `Post` object (`...rest`) directly into the Prisma `PostCreateInput`. The domain `Post` object carries a field named **`status`** (a `PostStatus` enum string like `'DRAFT'` or `'PUBLISHED'`). The Prisma schema, however, does **not** have a `status` column — it uses a **`isPublished` Boolean** field (mapped to `is_published` in the DB).

Because `status` is not a recognised Prisma field, Prisma silently ignores it. Since no `isPublished` value is explicitly provided in the spread, Prisma falls back to the schema-level `@default(false)` — which would actually be correct. **But** the spread also carries no `isPublished` key at all, so Prisma uses its default. This part is fine.

The real disaster is in **Bug 2**.

---

### Bug 2 — `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`, `PostMapper.toDomain` (lines 77–88)

**File:** `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`
**Function:** `PostMapper.toDomain`

```typescript
// CURRENT (buggy)
toDomain(data: Prisma.PostUncheckedCreateInput & { createdAt: Date }): Post {
  return {
    id: data.id,
    title: data.title,
    content: data.content,
    authorId: data.authorId,
    tags: (data.tags as string[]) ?? [],
    published: data.published,   // ← BUG: wrong field name read from Prisma record
    createdAt: data.createdAt,
    updatedAt: data.updatedAt ?? data.createdAt,
  };
},
```

The Prisma schema names the field **`isPublished`** (line 123 of `schema.prisma`). The mapper reads it as **`data.published`** — a field that does not exist on the Prisma record. In JavaScript/TypeScript, accessing a non-existent property returns `undefined`. The domain `Post` object is therefore reconstructed with `published: undefined`.

---

### Bug 3 — `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`, `PrismaRepository.updatePost` (lines 156–171)

**File:** `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`
**Function:** `PrismaRepository.updatePost`

```typescript
// CURRENT (buggy)
await prisma.post.update({
  where: { id: post.id },
  data: {
    title: post.title,
    content: post.content,
    tags: post.tags,
    published: post.published,   // ← BUG: wrong field name written to Prisma
    updatedAt: post.updatedAt ?? new Date(),
  },
});
```

Again, `published` is not the Prisma field name — it is `isPublished`. This means updates to the published state are silently dropped on every `updatePost` call.

---

### Bug 4 — `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`, `PrismaRepository.listPublishedPosts` (lines 186–194)

**File:** `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`
**Function:** `PrismaRepository.listPublishedPosts`

```typescript
// CURRENT (buggy)
const records = await prisma.post.findMany({
  where: { published: true },   // ← BUG: wrong field name in query filter
  ...
});
```

The filter `{ published: true }` references a non-existent Prisma field. Prisma will either throw a runtime error or — depending on the version — ignore the unknown key and return **all posts** regardless of their published state, making draft posts publicly visible.

---

## Summary of All Changes Required

### File: `src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`

| Location | Current (buggy) | Correct fix |
|---|---|---|
| `PostMapper.toPrismaCreate` (line 72–75) | `const { id, ...rest } = data; return { id, ...rest };` | Explicitly map domain fields to Prisma fields, translating `status === PostStatus.PUBLISHED` → `isPublished: true/false` |
| `PostMapper.toDomain` (line 84) | `published: data.published` | `isPublished: data.isPublished` (and rename the field in the domain `Post` type accordingly, or map it to `status`) |
| `PrismaRepository.updatePost` (line 164) | `published: post.published` | `isPublished: post.isPublished` (or the correctly mapped boolean) |
| `PrismaRepository.listPublishedPosts` (line 188) | `where: { published: true }` | `where: { isPublished: true }` |

#### Concrete corrected code for `PostMapper`:

```typescript
// FIXED
const PostMapper = {
  toPrismaCreate(data: Post): Prisma.PostCreateInput {
    return {
      id:          data.id,
      title:       data.title,
      content:     data.content,
      authorId:    data.authorId,
      tags:        data.tags,
      isPublished: data.status === 'PUBLISHED',   // ← translate enum → boolean
      publishedAt: data.publishedAt ?? null,
      createdAt:   data.createdAt,
      updatedAt:   data.updatedAt,
    };
  },

  toDomain(data: Prisma.PostUncheckedCreateInput & { createdAt: Date }): Post {
    return {
      id:          data.id,
      title:       data.title,
      content:     data.content,
      authorId:    data.authorId,
      tags:        (data.tags as string[]) ?? [],
      status:      data.isPublished ? 'PUBLISHED' : 'DRAFT',  // ← correct field name
      publishedAt: (data.publishedAt as Date | null) ?? null,
      createdAt:   data.createdAt,
      updatedAt:   (data.updatedAt as Date) ?? data.createdAt,
    };
  },
};
```

#### Concrete corrected code for `updatePost`:

```typescript
// FIXED
await prisma.post.update({
  where: { id: post.id },
  data: {
    title:       post.title,
    content:     post.content,
    tags:        post.tags,
    isPublished: post.status === 'PUBLISHED',   // ← correct field name
    publishedAt: post.publishedAt ?? null,
    updatedAt:   post.updatedAt ?? new Date(),
  },
});
```

#### Concrete corrected code for `listPublishedPosts`:

```typescript
// FIXED
const records = await prisma.post.findMany({
  where: { isPublished: true },   // ← correct field name
  orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },
  take: limit,
  skip: offset,
});
```

---

## Why This Caused the Exact Symptom

The chain of failures is:

1. A user creates a post with `status: 'DRAFT'`.
2. `PostMapper.toPrismaCreate` spreads the domain object blindly — `isPublished` is never set, so Prisma uses `@default(false)` → the row is correctly stored as not-published in the DB.
3. The created row is returned and passed through `PostMapper.toDomain`, which reads `data.published` (undefined) instead of `data.isPublished` (false). The reconstructed in-memory `Post` has `published: undefined`.
4. `listPublishedPosts` queries `where: { published: true }` — an unknown field — causing Prisma to return **all rows** (no effective filter), so draft posts appear in the public listing.
5. Any subsequent `updatePost` call writes `published: post.published` (undefined → ignored by Prisma), so the `isPublished` column is never updated even when a legitimate publish action is taken.

The fix is entirely contained in **`src/adapters/outbound/prisma.repository.ts`**: replace every occurrence of the incorrect field name `published` with the correct Prisma model field name `isPublished`, and add an explicit translation between the domain's `status` string enum and the persistence layer's boolean column.

